---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Model Selection - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="model-selection">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Not all models are the same, and picking the right one for a task matters. The difference
				between models isn't just "smarter vs. dumber"; they have genuinely different strengths,
				weaknesses, speeds, and costs. But here's the thing: nobody can tell you which model is
				best for <em>your</em> work. That depends on your prompting style, your tasks, and your
				tolerance for waiting.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Tip: don't be afraid to start over">
				<p>
					If a model takes your code in the wrong direction, it's almost always faster to revert
					the changes (<code>git checkout</code>,{" "}
					<code>git stash</code>, or just start
					a new chat) than to ask the AI to undo what it did. Trying to talk a model into walking
					back its own changes often creates more mess. A clean slate is cheap; use it.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				Start Fast and Cheap, Then Scale Up
			</h3>

			<p>
				The most practical approach to model selection is simple: <strong class="text-stone-800"
					>start with the fastest, cheapest model and only move up when you hit its limits</strong
				>. Most people do the opposite. They reach for the most powerful model by default and
				never question it. That's like driving a truck to get groceries. It works, but it's slow
				and expensive for what you actually need.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<Callout variant="tip" title="Step 1: Try a small, fast model first">
					<p>
						For any new task, start with one of the cheaper, faster models (think Haiku-tier or
						equivalent). You'll be surprised how often they handle things perfectly. Renaming
						variables, generating boilerplate, explaining code, simple refactors: a lightweight
						model handles these just fine, and the faster response time keeps you in flow.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Step 2: Move up when you notice gaps">
					<p>
						If the output isn't good enough (the model misses nuance, gets logic wrong, or
						struggles with complex multi-step reasoning), step up to a mid-tier model. This is
						where you'll land for most everyday development work: writing functions, debugging,
						working through moderately complex problems.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Step 3: Use the heavy hitters for genuinely hard problems">
					<p>
						Save the most capable (and expensive) models for tasks that truly demand it: designing
						complex architectures, reviewing code for subtle bugs, working through difficult
						technical decisions, or handling large amounts of context. You'll know when you need
						them because the smaller models will visibly struggle.
					</p>
				</Callout>
			</div>

			<h3>
				Plan Big, Execute Cheap
			</h3>

			<p>
				The escalation advice above is about picking one model for a task. The other half of the current wisdom is splitting a single task <em>across</em> models by role: use a strong reasoning model to plan and to review, and hand the actual execution to a cheaper, faster one. A plan detailed enough for a junior developer to follow turns out to be detailed enough for a cheaper model to execute well, and reviewing the diff with a strong model again catches what the executor missed.
			</p>

			<p>
				This is where the real cost savings live. Reserving the expensive model for the hard thinking (planning and judging) while a cheap model does the bulk typing can cut cost dramatically compared to running everything on the top-tier model. Mitchell Hashimoto, creator of Ghostty and founder of HashiCorp, described his setup:
			</p>

			<div class="my-8 max-w-xl border border-stone-200 rounded-2xl bg-white px-5 py-4 shadow-sm not-prose">
				<div class="flex items-start gap-3">
					<div
						class="w-11 h-11 rounded-full bg-stone-800 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-base font-semibold shrink-0"
						style="font-family: 'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;"
					>
						M
					</div>
					<div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
						<div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-2">
							<div class="leading-tight">
								<div class="flex items-center gap-1">
									<span class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-stone-900">Mitchell Hashimoto</span>
									<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-label="Verified account" class="w-[18px] h-[18px] text-sky-500 fill-current">
										<path
											d="M22.25 12c0-1.43-.88-2.67-2.19-3.34.46-1.39.2-2.9-.81-3.91s-2.52-1.27-3.91-.81c-.66-1.31-1.91-2.19-3.34-2.19s-2.67.88-3.33 2.19c-1.4-.46-2.91-.2-3.92.81s-1.26 2.52-.8 3.91c-1.31.67-2.2 1.91-2.2 3.34s.89 2.67 2.2 3.34c-.46 1.39-.21 2.9.8 3.91s2.52 1.26 3.91.81c.67 1.31 1.91 2.19 3.34 2.19s2.68-.88 3.34-2.19c1.39.45 2.9.2 3.91-.81s1.27-2.52.81-3.91c1.31-.67 2.19-1.91 2.19-3.34zm-11.71 4.2L6.8 12.46l1.41-1.42 2.26 2.26 4.8-5.23 1.47 1.36-6.2 6.77z"
										></path>
									</svg>
								</div>
								<span class="text-sm text-stone-500">@mitchellh</span>
							</div>
							<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-label="X" class="w-5 h-5 text-stone-900 fill-current shrink-0">
								<path
									d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"
								></path>
							</svg>
						</div>
						<p class="mt-2 text-[15px] text-stone-900 leading-normal m-0">
							I'm having a lot of success using Fable xhigh as a planner/architect, using
							GPT 5.5 xhigh (subscription) as a coder, then Fable xhigh again as a judge. At API
							pricing, planning+judge costs are in the ~few dollar range compared to typical $50+
							full round trips.
						</p>
						<div class="mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-stone-100">
							<a
								href="https://x.com/mitchellh/status/2072715852944957531"
								target="_blank"
								rel="noopener noreferrer"
								class="text-sm text-stone-500 no-underline hover:underline"
							>
								View on X
							</a>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				Take the roles as the lasting idea, not the model names. This space moves so fast that
				Hashimoto himself has since switched his default planner/coder to a newer model. The pattern
				(a strong model plans and judges, a cheap one executes) is what holds. Many tools now make
				this easy: sub-agents, per-mode model settings in Claude Code, and Cursor's model picker all
				let you assign different models to different stages of the work.
			</p>

			<p>
				So the two ideas fit together: for a simple, well-scoped task, just use a small model. For
				anything with real design in it, plan with a strong model, let a cheaper one do the bulk of
				the work, and review with the strong model again.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Every Model Responds Differently to You
			</h3>

			<p>
				Here's something that isn't discussed enough: different models respond differently to the
				same prompt. The way you phrase things, the context you provide, the level of detail in
				your instructions. These interact with each model's training in unique ways. A prompt
				that works brilliantly with one model might get mediocre results from another.
			</p>

			<p>
				This means you need to <strong class="text-stone-800">experiment</strong>. When you find
				a model that clicks with how you naturally communicate, you'll get better results with
				less effort. Don't just take someone else's recommendation, try a few models on your
				actual work and see which one fits your style.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="This changes fast">
				<p>
					Model rankings shift every few months. A model that's best at coding today might be
					second-best next quarter. The specific models available when you read this will be
					different from when this was written. When a new model comes out, feel free to experiment with it and see if it works better for you.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				The Three Levers
			</h3>

			<p>
				When comparing models, you're really weighing three things: <strong
					class="text-stone-800">capability</strong
				>, <strong class="text-stone-800">speed</strong>, and <strong class="text-stone-800"
					>cost</strong
				>. These trade off against each other. Most providers offer a range of tiers (think: small/medium/large) that let you
				pick your tradeoff point.
			</p>

			<h3>
				A Note on Cost
			</h3>

			<p>
				AI model pricing is based on tokens processed (input) and tokens generated (output). Larger,
				more capable models cost more per token. However, more capable models may process the same task in fewer tokens, so the total cost may be lower.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Tracking Model Performance
			</h3>

			<p>
				Because the landscape shifts so frequently, it helps to have go-to resources for comparing
				models objectively. These sites run standardized benchmarks and aggregate real-world
				performance data:
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<a
					href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/leaderboards/models"
					target="_blank"
					rel="noopener noreferrer"
					class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 hover:border-orange-300 hover:bg-orange-50/30 transition-colors group"
				>
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 group-hover:text-orange-700 mb-1">
						Artificial Analysis: Model Leaderboard
					</div>
					<p class="text-xs text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
						Comprehensive comparison of 100+ models across intelligence, speed, price, and context
						window size. Great for comparing the cost-performance tradeoff between models and
						providers side by side.
					</p>
				</a>

				<a
					href="https://lmarena.ai/"
					target="_blank"
					rel="noopener noreferrer"
					class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 hover:border-orange-300 hover:bg-orange-50/30 transition-colors group"
				>
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 group-hover:text-orange-700 mb-1">
						Chatbot Arena (LMArena)
					</div>
					<p class="text-xs text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
						Community-driven blind comparison where users vote between anonymous model responses.
						The Elo-style rankings reflect real human preference rather than synthetic benchmarks.
						Includes separate leaderboards for coding, reasoning, and general tasks.
					</p>
				</a>

				<a
					href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/models"
					target="_blank"
					rel="noopener noreferrer"
					class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 hover:border-orange-300 hover:bg-orange-50/30 transition-colors group"
				>
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 group-hover:text-orange-700 mb-1">
						Artificial Analysis: Model Comparison
					</div>
					<p class="text-xs text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
						Detailed head-to-head model comparison with pricing breakdowns, throughput benchmarks,
						and latency measurements across different API providers. Useful when you're deciding
						which provider to use for a specific model.
					</p>
				</a>

				<a
					href="https://llm-stats.com/"
					target="_blank"
					rel="noopener noreferrer"
					class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 hover:border-orange-300 hover:bg-orange-50/30 transition-colors group"
				>
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 group-hover:text-orange-700 mb-1">
						LLM Stats: AI Leaderboards
					</div>
					<p class="text-xs text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
						Aggregates rankings from multiple benchmark sources into a single view. Good for a
						quick snapshot of where models stand across different evaluation criteria.
					</p>
				</a>
			</div>

			<p>
				No single benchmark tells the whole story. A model that tops the coding leaderboard might
				rank lower on general reasoning. One that's fastest might sacrifice accuracy. The best
				approach is to check a couple of these resources, understand the tradeoffs, and then test
				with your actual tasks.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="The practical takeaway">
				<p>
					Start with the smallest model that might work, and only upgrade when you see it fall
					short. Experiment with different models on your real tasks, not examples. Pay
					attention to which models match your communication style. The goal isn't to find "the
					best model"; it's to find the best model for you and your specific tasks.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
